[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **None.** Every input class enumerated below produced byte-identical stdout,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte-identical stderr and an identical exit status on the first comparison. This
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m section is kept deliberately explicit: the empty result is the finding, not an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m omission.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two candidate mismatches were investigated up front because they are the usual
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m failure modes for this shape of program. Both turned out to be already handled
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m correctly by the existing Rust code, so neither is a fix — they are recorded
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m because they are the parts a future edit is most likely to break.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **Out-of-`int`-range input is *not* clamped to `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX`.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    glibc converts the digit run for `%d` with `strtol` (a `long`) and then
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    narrows the result to `int`, so `2147483648` prints `00000080` (`INT_MIN`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and `4294967296` prints `00000000`. A translation that used
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `str::parse::<i32>()` — or that saturated at `i32` bounds — would differ on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    every input above `INT_MAX`. The Rust code accumulates into a wider type and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    performs the same narrowing (`as_long as i32`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Covered by `int_boundaries_and_narrowing`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **Saturation happens at `long` range, before the narrowing.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `strtol` clamps to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`, and *that* clamped value is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    truncated. So any digit run above `2^63-1` prints `ffffffff` (`LONG_MAX`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    truncated to `-1`) and any below `-2^63` prints `00000000` (`LONG_MIN`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    truncated to `0`) — note the asymmetry. `99999999999999999999999999999`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    gives `ffffffff` while its negation gives `00000000`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Covered by `long_range_saturation`.
